  "response": "## Most likely cause\n\n`src/billing.ts:92` — the loyalty-discount day count inside `generateInvoice`:\n\n```ts\nconst daysSince = Math.floor((Date.now() - user.createdAt) / (1000 * 60 * 60 * 24));\nif (user.plan !== \"free\") {\n  if (daysSince > 365)      discountCents = Math.round(subtotal * 0.1);\n  else if (daysSince > 180) discountCents = Math.round(subtotal * 0.05);\n  else if (daysSince > 90)  discountCents = Math.round(subtotal * 0.02);\n}\n```\n\nThree separate defects stack into exactly the \"passes most days, fails on a boundary\" signature:\n\n1. **Unpinned wall clock.** `crime_00012` (Temporal Recklessness, `src/billing.ts`, lines 57/63/92/198/238/250/273). Nothing injects a clock, so `user.createdAt` in a fixture is anchored to one instant and `Date.now()` at line 92 to another.\n2. **`Math.floor` at an exclusive threshold.** A fixture meaning \"365 days old\" lands on `daysSince === 365`, and `> 365` is false. The tier boundary is off by one in a way that only shows up for fixtures built *at* a threshold.\n3. **A day is assumed to be exactly 86,400,000 ms.** A fixture built as `Date.now() - 365 * 24 * 60 * 60 * 1000` spans a DST transition in most timezones, so it is 364.96 or 365.04 *calendar* days of wall time. `floor` turns that into 364 or 365 depending on the host timezone and the date the suite runs. The discount flips between 0% and 10%, and `discountCents` → `afterDiscount` → tax → `totalCents` all move with it.\n\nItem 3 is the one that makes it *flaky* rather than merely wrong: the failure is seasonal and TZ-dependent, not deterministic.\n\nWorth noting: `generateInvoice` reads the clock five separate times (57, 63, 92, 198, 238/250). Two reads inside one invoice can straddle a boundary even in a single run.\n\n## What I ruled out, and why\n\n**`src/date.ts` is a red herring here, despite outscoring billing.ts.** The three highest-signal temporal findings — `crime_00009` (Half-UTC, Half-Local, `high`), `crime_00011` (Timezone Roulette), `crime_00018` (DST-Naive Day Math) — are all in `src/date.ts`. That file is genuinely full of date-boundary landmines. But nothing imports it: `grep -rn 'from \"./date\"' src/` returns nothing, and `src/billing.ts` has no imports at all. It cannot be in the billing tests' path. Ranking by severity alone would have sent you to the wrong file.\n\n**The committed billing tests can't produce this failure.** `crime_00025` and `crime_00031` say so, and the source confirms it — `src/billing.test.ts:5` calls a no-op local helper with zero assertions, and line 9 asserts `toBeTruthy()` against a hardcoded `{ id: \"invoice_123\" }` that never touches `generateInvoice`. Whatever is failing is a test not in this file, or newly written against the real function.\n\n**A second-order suspect worth a look:** `crime_00033` (Contract Split-Brain) reports that `Invoice` in `src/api/state.ts:9` is missing `createdAt`, `currency`, and the four money fields that `src/billing.ts:19` declares, and `crime_00032` reports the same for `User.createdAt`. If a test builds its user from the `api/state.ts` shape, `user.createdAt` is `undefined`, `daysSince` is `NaN`, every comparison is false, and the discount silently becomes 0. That's a deterministic wrong answer rather than a flake, but it's the same field, so check the fixture's provenance while you're in there.\n\n## Proposed fix\n\nInject a clock, read it once per invocation, and centralise the day math. Smallest change that removes the nondeterminism:\n\n```ts\n// src/billing.ts\nexport interface Clock {\n  now(): number;\n}\nexport const systemClock: Clock = { now: () => Date.now() };\n\nconst MS_PER_DAY = 86_400_000;\n\n/** Whole days elapsed, floor-rounded. Callers own the tier boundaries. */\nexport function accountAgeDays(createdAt: number, nowMs: number): number {\n  return Math.floor((nowMs - createdAt) / MS_PER_DAY);\n}\n```\n\nThen in `generateInvoice`, take `clock: Clock = systemClock` as a parameter, read `const nowMs = clock.now()` **once** at the top, and use that single value for lines 57, 63, 92, 198, 238 and 250. Do the same in `recommendUpgrade` (`src/billing.ts:273`), which carries a byte-identical copy of the expression — the comment at line 91 already admits the duplication, and today the two copies can disagree within one request.\n\nTwo decisions to make explicit while you're there, because the current code leaves both implicit:\n\n- **Boundary inclusivity.** Decide whether \"365 days old\" earns the 10% tier and write `>= 365` or `> 365` deliberately. Add a test at exactly the boundary and one at boundary-minus-one-ms.\n- **Calendar vs. elapsed days.** If the business rule means *calendar* days in the user's timezone, `MS_PER_DAY` arithmetic is wrong regardless of the clock injection, and you need a calendar-aware operation (`Temporal`, Luxon, `date-fns-tz`) — this is `crime_00018`'s `use_calendar_aware_lib` action applied to billing rather than to `date.ts`. If it means *elapsed* 24-hour periods, the ms math is correct and the fixtures are what need fixing.\n\nTests then pass `{ now: () => FIXED_MS }` and the flake is structurally impossible.\n\n## One caveat on the scan itself\n\nTwo places where the report undersold the actual risk, worth knowing before you trust the ranking on the next run:\n\n- `crime_00012` states *\"none decide a branch — all 7 record or render the reading\"* and scores `test_gap: 0`, `severity: medium`. That's true syntactically — no `Date.now()` sits inside a comparison — but the line-92 reading is one hop from four branches via `daysSince`. The detector appears to test for a clock read *directly* in a comparison, so a single intermediate binding hides it. The same detector correctly rates `src/date.ts:4` as branch-deciding because `Date.now() > timestampMs` is inline.\n- `crime_00018` caught the literal `86400000` at `src/date.ts:52`, in the unused `tomorrowMs`, but missed the same day constant at `src/billing.ts:92` and `:273` where it's load-bearing — presumably because it's spelled `(1000 * 60 * 60 * 24)` rather than as a single literal.\n\nThe scan still pointed at the right file and the right lines, just with the severity ordering inverted relative to the actual bug.",
